  "outcomeTags": {
    "required": "The outcome follows from an ENV §3 MUST, a VEC rule, or CBOR well-formedness (RFC 8949 §3 and Appendix F), which ENV §3 presupposes. An implementation under test that differs FAILS the case.",
    "labChoice": "The texts leave the outcome open. The case records the laboratory decoder's choice; an implementation under test that differs is reported, and never FAILS on it.",
    "informative": "The strict decoder's outcome is a laboratory choice beyond ENV §3 (decision cbor-det-v1-decoder). It is recorded for the suites that read maps strictly and is never an expectation under SDX-A-01."
  },
  "decisions": [
    {
      "id": "cbor-det-v1-profile",
      "kind": "Rests on the cited text",
      "text": "The encoder follows the published `cbor-det-v1` vectors where they and the ENV §3 prose differ, as VEC directs: safe integers (±(2^53 - 1)) take the shortest integer head; every other number, including integral values beyond the safe range, is binary64 (vectors `int-above-safe-range-stays-float64`, `int-far-above-safe-range-stays-float64`); bigint inputs are refused. Map keys are ordered by their encoded bytes; absent map members are elided, absent array elements become null, and an absent whole value is CBOR undefined (ENV §3)."
    },
    {
      "id": "cbor-det-v1-specials",
      "kind": "Laboratory interpretation",
      "text": "The encoder writes -0 as integer 0, NaN as fb7ff8000000000000 and ±Infinity as binary64. The published vectors do not pin these values, and the laboratory cross-checked them against cbor-x 1.6.9, where the published suite names cbor-x 1.6.4 (coverage gap ENV-3)."
    },
    {
      "id": "cbor-det-v1-decoder",
      "kind": "Rests on the cited text",
      "text": "The semantic decoder implements ENV §3: it rejects tags and indefinite-length items, accepts non-shortest heads, and returns binary64 values as numbers, so the historical-float64-decode bytes read back to the integers shown. It rejects input that is not well-formed CBOR (RFC 8949 §3, Appendix F). As laboratory choices it also rejects well-formed input outside the profile: integers beyond ±(2^53 - 1), maps with duplicate keys and text that is not valid UTF-8 (both not valid, RFC 8949 §5.3.1, §5.6), and unassigned simple values. The strict decoder additionally requires the input to be the deterministic encoding of the decoded value."
    },
    {
      "id": "lab-bounds",
      "kind": "Laboratory test-construction choice",
      "text": "The checker bounds nesting depth at 64 and input at 16 MiB, refuses duplicate set members and text containing a lone surrogate on encode. ENV §17.6 requires bounds and fixes no values, so these are laboratory bounds. A refusal caused by one of them when reading an implementation's output is reported as ERROR, never FAIL."
    },
    {
      "id": "thin-suite",
      "kind": "Laboratory test-construction choice",
      "text": "Cases VA-ENC-001 to 044 reference the published SDX vectors by file and name and record this implementation's output; the expected bytes and values are those of the published files, which are stored unchanged and pinned by SHA-256. Cases VA-ENC-101 to 126 are laboratory-derived decoder cases, and VA-ENC-201 onward laboratory-derived encoder cases; each carries its `basis` tag and the clause it rests on."
    }
  ],
